{"content": "\nYou are an AI assistant that helps a human analyst to perform general information discovery. Information discovery is the process of identifying and assessing relevant information associated with certain entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n3478,STATISTICAL APPROACH,The statistical approach to designing experiments involves various methodologies and techniques for conducting experiments and analyzing data.,6\r\n603,REGRESSION MODELS,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Regression Models**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nRegression models are statistical processes designed to estimate the relationships among variables, primarily focusing on predicting the value of a dependent variable based on one or more independent variables. These models serve as essential tools in data analysis and predictive modeling, allowing researchers and analysts to understand how changes in predictor variables influence the outcome variable.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe general mathematical formulation of a regression model can be expressed as:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + ... + \\beta_nX_n + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( Y \\) is the dependent variable,\n- \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept,\n- \\( \\beta_1, \\beta_2, ..., \\beta_n \\) are the coefficients of the independent variables \\( X_1, X_2, ..., X_n \\),\n- \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nRegression models can be structured in various forms, including:\n- **Simple Linear Regression:** Involves one independent variable and one dependent variable.\n- **Multiple Linear Regression:** Involves two or more independent variables.\n- **Polynomial Regression:** Involves independent variables raised to a power.\n- **Logistic Regression:** Used for binary dependent variables.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nTo ensure the validity of regression models, several key assumptions must be met:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables is linear.\n2. Independence: Observations are independent of each other.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of the residuals is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. Normality: The residuals of the model are normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nRegression models are applicable when the goal is to understand the relationship between variables and to make predictions. They are particularly useful when:\n- The dependent variable is continuous.\n- The researcher seeks to quantify the effect of one or more predictors.\n- The assumptions of linearity and normality are reasonably satisfied.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure for regression models typically involves:\n1. Specifying the model structure.\n2. Estimating the coefficients using methods such as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS).\n3. Evaluating the model fit using metrics like R-squared, Adjusted R-squared, and residual analysis.\n4. Conducting hypothesis tests on the coefficients to determine their significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Regression models provide a clear framework for understanding relationships between variables.\n- They allow for the quantification of the strength and direction of these relationships.\n- The models can be easily extended to include interaction terms and polynomial terms for more complex relationships.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Regression models can be sensitive to outliers, which may skew results.\n- They rely heavily on the assumptions mentioned earlier; violations can lead to misleading conclusions.\n- Overfitting can occur when too many predictors are included, reducing the model's generalizability.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methods include:\n- Generalized Linear Models (GLMs) for non-normal response variables.\n- Time Series Analysis for data collected over time.\n- Machine Learning techniques such as decision trees and neural networks for more complex relationships.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing regression models, practitioners should consider:\n- The selection of appropriate independent variables based on theoretical understanding and exploratory data analysis.\n- The need for data preprocessing, including handling missing values and scaling variables.\n- The importance of validating the model using techniques such as cross-validation.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nRegression models can be implemented using various statistical software packages, including R, Python (with libraries such as scikit-learn and statsmodels), SAS, and SPSS. These tools provide built-in functions for model fitting, diagnostics, and visualization.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nRegression models are widely used across various fields, including:\n- Economics for predicting consumer spending.\n- Medicine for assessing the impact of treatments on health outcomes.\n- Marketing for analyzing the effectiveness of advertising campaigns.\n- Environmental science for modeling the relationship between pollutants and health effects. \n\nIn summary, regression models are versatile statistical tools that facilitate the understanding and prediction of relationships among variables, provided that their assumptions are adequately addressed and the models are appropriately specified.\",7\r\n155,PARAMETERS,\"**Summary of Parameters in Statistical Models and Experimental Designs**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nParameters are numerical characteristics that define the behavior and structure of statistical models and experimental designs. They serve as essential components that influence outcomes and interpretations in various statistical analyses, including regression models and block designs. Parameters can include values such as means, variances, coefficients, and structural elements of designs.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn statistical models, parameters often take the form of coefficients in regression equations, which can be represented as:\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + ... + \\beta_nX_n + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the dependent variable, \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the parameters (coefficients) for independent variables \\( X_i \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term. In the context of experimental designs, parameters define the structure, such as the number of treatments, blocks, and replications.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn the context of experimental designs, parameters specify the layout of the design. For instance, in a Randomized Block Design (RBD), parameters include the number of blocks and treatments. In a Partially Balanced Incomplete Block Design (PBIBD), parameters define the number of treatments, blocks, and replicates, which dictate how treatments are allocated across blocks.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of parameters in statistical models and designs relies on several assumptions, including:\n- Independence of observations\n- Normality of residuals\n- Homogeneity of variances (homoscedasticity)\n- Correct specification of the model\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nParameters are applicable in various statistical analyses, including regression analysis, ANOVA, and experimental design. The selection of parameters depends on the research question, the nature of the data, and the specific design requirements. For example, in block designs, the number of treatments and blocks must be carefully considered to ensure adequate power and control of variability.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis involving parameters typically includes estimating the parameters from the data using methods such as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) for regression models or ANOVA techniques for experimental designs. The estimated parameters are then used to make inferences about the population or to predict outcomes.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe use of parameters allows for a structured approach to modeling and analyzing data. They provide a clear framework for understanding relationships between variables and for designing experiments that can yield reliable and valid results. Parameters also facilitate the quantification of uncertainty and variability in statistical analyses.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nOne limitation of parameters is that they can be sensitive to model specification. Incorrectly specified models can lead to biased parameter estimates. Additionally, the complexity of models can increase with the number of parameters, potentially leading to overfitting, especially in small sample sizes.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nParameters are integral to various designs and methods, including Completely Randomized Designs (CRD), Factorial Designs, and Response Surface Methodology (RSM). Each of these approaches has its own set of parameters that define their structure and analysis.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing parameters in statistical analyses, researchers must ensure that the chosen parameters align with the experimental design and the underlying assumptions of the statistical methods used. Careful planning and consideration of sample size, treatment allocation, and replication are crucial for obtaining valid results.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nParameters can be estimated and analyzed using various statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python libraries. These tools provide functions for fitting models, conducting ANOVA, and performing regression analyses, allowing researchers to efficiently work with parameters in their data.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nParameters are widely used in fields such as agriculture, medicine, and social sciences. For example, in agricultural experiments, parameters may define the number of different fertilizer treatments applied to crops, while in clinical trials, parameters may represent the dosage levels of a drug being tested. In both cases, the careful selection and estimation of parameters are critical for drawing valid conclusions from the data.\",8\r\n1864,COEFFICIENTS,\"**Summary of Coefficients in Statistical Models**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nCoefficients are numerical values that quantify the strength and direction of the relationship between variables in a statistical model. In the context of regression analysis, coefficients serve to express how changes in independent variables are associated with changes in a dependent variable, thereby providing insights into the nature of these relationships.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**  \nIn a typical linear regression model, the relationship can be expressed as:  \n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + ... + \\beta_kX_k + \\epsilon \\]  \nwhere \\( Y \\) is the dependent variable, \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_1, \\beta_2, ..., \\beta_k \\) are the coefficients for the independent variables \\( X_1, X_2, ..., X_k \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term. Each coefficient \\( \\beta_i \\) indicates the expected change in \\( Y \\) for a one-unit increase in \\( X_i \\), holding all other variables constant.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nWhile coefficients themselves do not have a design layout, they are derived from data collected through various experimental designs, such as completely randomized designs, factorial designs, or block designs, where the relationships between independent and dependent variables are assessed.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of coefficients in regression models relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between independent and dependent variables is linear.\n2. Independence: Observations are independent of each other.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of residuals is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. Normality: The residuals of the model are normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nCoefficients are applicable in situations where the researcher seeks to understand the relationship between one or more independent variables and a dependent variable. They are particularly useful in predictive modeling, hypothesis testing, and when assessing the impact of various factors on an outcome.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nTo estimate coefficients, one typically employs methods such as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression. The analysis involves fitting the model to the data, estimating the coefficients, and evaluating their significance through hypothesis tests (e.g., t-tests) and confidence intervals.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Coefficients provide clear, interpretable metrics of relationships between variables.\n- They allow for the quantification of effects, facilitating decision-making and predictions.\n- Regression models can accommodate multiple independent variables, enabling complex analyses.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- Coefficients can be misleading if the underlying assumptions of the regression model are violated.\n- They do not imply causation; correlation does not equate to causation.\n- Multicollinearity among independent variables can distort coefficient estimates and their interpretations.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nCoefficients are integral to various regression techniques, including multiple regression, logistic regression, and polynomial regression. Alternatives to regression analysis include non-parametric methods and machine learning techniques, which may not rely on coefficients in the traditional sense.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing models to derive coefficients, it is crucial to ensure that the data meets the assumptions of the chosen statistical method. Data preprocessing, including handling missing values and outliers, is often necessary to improve model performance.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nCoefficients can be estimated using statistical software packages such as R, Python (with libraries like scikit-learn and statsmodels), SAS, and SPSS, which provide built-in functions for regression analysis and output detailed summaries of coefficient estimates.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nCoefficients are widely used in fields such as economics, social sciences, health sciences, and marketing. For example, in a study examining the impact of education and experience on salary, the coefficients would indicate how much salary is expected to increase with each additional year of education or experience.\",1\r\n3480,SAMPLE SIZE DETERMINATION,Sample size determination is the process of calculating the number of observations or replicates needed in a study to ensure reliable results.,2\r\n3477,KNOVEL,\"Knovel is a platform that provides access to a wide range of technical information and resources, including statistical methods and experimental designs.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n4487,STATISTICAL APPROACH,ANOVA,The statistical approach often includes methods like ANOVA for analyzing variance among groups,169\r\n661,REGRESSION MODELS,RESPONSE SURFACE METHODOLOGY,Regression Models are often employed within Response Surface Methodology to model and analyze the relationships between factors and responses.,26\r\n517,PBIBD,PARAMETERS,\"### Comprehensive Summary of PBIBD (Partially Balanced Incomplete Block Design) and Its Parameters\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nThe Partially Balanced Incomplete Block Design (PBIBD) is a type of experimental design used when it is impractical or impossible to test all treatments in every block. PBIBD allows for a systematic arrangement of treatments into blocks, where each block contains a subset of treatments, facilitating the study of treatment effects while controlling for variability among experimental units. The primary purpose of PBIBD is to provide a balanced approach to experimentation, ensuring that each treatment is compared under similar conditions while accommodating the constraints of incomplete blocking.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn a PBIBD, treatments are assigned to blocks such that each treatment appears in a specified number of blocks, and the number of treatments in each block is less than the total number of treatments. The design is characterized by parameters such as:\n- \\( v \\): the total number of treatments,\n- \\( b \\): the total number of blocks,\n- \\( k \\): the number of treatments per block,\n- \\( r \\): the number of times each treatment appears across all blocks,\n- \\( \\lambda \\): the number of blocks in which each pair of treatments appears together.\n\nThe model can be expressed in terms of the treatment effects and block effects, typically using a linear model framework.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nThe structure of a PBIBD is defined by its parameters, which dictate how treatments are arranged within blocks. Each block contains a subset of treatments, and the arrangement is designed to ensure that certain pairs of treatments are replicated across blocks, albeit not all pairs. This partial balance allows for the estimation of treatment effects while controlling for block effects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nFor the PBIBD to yield valid results, several assumptions must be met:\n1. The experimental units within blocks are homogeneous.\n2. The treatment effects are additive and independent.\n3. The errors are normally distributed and have constant variance.\n4. The blocks are representative of the experimental units.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nPBIBD is particularly useful in situations where:\n- The number of treatments is large relative to the number of experimental units.\n- It is not feasible to test all treatments in every block due to resource constraints.\n- Researchers wish to control for variability among experimental units while still obtaining information about treatment effects.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of PBIBD typically involves the use of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to assess the significance of treatment effects while accounting for block effects. The ANOVA model will partition the total variability into components attributable to treatments, blocks, and error.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Efficient use of resources by allowing incomplete blocking.\n- Flexibility in treatment arrangement, accommodating various experimental constraints.\n- Ability to estimate treatment effects with reduced bias from block variability.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Complexity in design and analysis compared to complete designs.\n- Potential for increased variability in treatment effect estimates due to incomplete pairing of treatments.\n- Requires careful planning to ensure that the design meets the necessary balance criteria.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nPBIBD is related to other incomplete block designs, such as Complete Block Designs (CBD) and Balanced Incomplete Block Designs (BIBD). Researchers may also consider factorial designs or Latin square designs depending on the specific experimental context and objectives.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing a PBIBD, researchers must carefully define the parameters and ensure that the design is feasible given the available resources. It is crucial to maintain the balance of treatment pairings to ensure valid statistical inferences.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, and Minitab provide tools for designing and analyzing PBIBDs. These tools can facilitate the generation of the design matrix and the execution of ANOVA procedures.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nPBIBDs are commonly used in agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and industrial applications where treatments cannot be fully replicated due to logistical constraints. For instance, in agricultural research, different crop varieties may be tested across various plots (blocks) where not all varieties can be planted in every plot due to space or resource limitations.\",18\r\n3088,COEFFICIENTS,PARAMETERS,\"Parameters are represented by coefficients in linear regression models, which indicate the strength and direction of relationships\",9\r\n4490,STATISTICAL APPROACH,SAMPLE SIZE DETERMINATION,The statistical approach includes sample size determination to ensure studies are adequately powered,8\r\n4486,KNOVEL,STATISTICAL APPROACH,Knovel provides resources and information related to the statistical approach to designing experiments,7\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\nOutput:", "role": "user"}], "parameters": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "frequency_penalty": 0.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1, "presence_penalty": 0.0, "temperature": 0.0, "top_p": 1.0, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}}}, "key": "chat_create_community_report_5a57b9c6716742466ecaffaa9a26bca2827f0d4c33025cf115226555506bb4f8_v2"}